title The compiler under everything answered a fair question with a segfault
prompt Take the next agent-doable lap Lindy-first crux-first, Standfast on reds first, deep-read council-rota row N mod 5, send each finished increment.
think Standfast opened the lap and the line was clear -- every living REDS row read CLOSED through %124, which the prior lap booked and closed in one round.
think The running thread was unambiguous. All three newest recommends name the same crux: main, standing byte-identical in 64 rungs at 17 lines, leading the carry queue's third seat and the ceiling pressure at once, with five lines of headroom under 87,800.
think The rota is row 1 this lap -- Saturn and air, law and boundary: TAME_CORE, single-stranded, Conway. Single-strandedness turned out to be the lap's whole argument, twice over, and I did not go looking for it.
think Measured the crux before touching it. The main family is 64 rungs at 17 lines, and beside it a SECOND family of 15 rungs at 17 lines differing in exactly one qualifier -- run_dependent against cohort.run_dependent. One lifted body serves all 79, so the fold pays 79 rungs rather than 64.
think Measured the widening cost across the WHOLE ladder rather than across the 79, which is last lap's thirteen-splits lesson applied before the split rather than after: run_self_test is 97 bodies all distinct, so widening it splits nothing; run_dependent is 68 bodies in three families that move together; fixed_argv_words is a const and the meter reads only fn bodies.
think Then the blocker. 35 of the 79 do not import ladder_checks yet, and four of those -- cohort, carry, cycle, unhand -- are modules ladder_checks itself imports. That is a mutual import, so I asked the compiler whether Rye allows one rather than reasoning about it.
think It answered with a segmentation fault. bridge_rye_tree recursed until the stack died.
think The root is exact and small. The visited set is appended AFTER the walk into a file's imports rather than before, so while a cycle is being traversed nothing is ever marked seen and bridge_already_staged can never fire. Post-order marking emits a topological order correctly and is worthless for termination; two duties had been folded into one list, which is exactly the braid single-strandedness argues against, sitting at the very bottom of the stack.
think Fixed it by marking on entry, and added a named bound beside it -- max_bridge_depth 1024, error.BridgeTooDeep with a printed diagnostic -- because TAME root rule 1 forbids unbounded recursion and a visited set alone still leaves depth unnamed.
think That exposed a second fault of the same family, visible only once the first was gone: the root file bridged to foo.rye.zig while every dependency bridged to foo.zig, so a module importing the root by name asked for a file the bridge had never written. One file, two names. Gave every file one name, root included.
think Proved both directions on metal rather than claiming either. The fixed compiler builds two files that import each other, links them, runs them, and a value crosses the cycle and returns unchanged. The previous binary segfaults with code 134 on that same corpus.
obs The deepest tool was the least-witnessed, and precisely because everything above it is green. 1,639 witnesses stand in this tree and not one was pointed at the compiler underneath every other -- every passing witness is evidence Rye works on the graphs the tree happens to hold, which is REDS %102's family at the bottom of the stack. Nothing in the tree holds a mutual .rye import, so no scan of the tree could ever have found this. Only a corpus written for the question reaches it.
obs The finding came from the crux rather than instead of it. I did not go hunting for a compiler bug; I tried to lift main, and the lift walked into the fault. A fold that wanted four base modules to import their own harness is the only thing in months that has asked Rye this question.
obs Registered the witness in the same lap it was written, per REDS %81, as rung 8/8 of the era suite -- the roster whose promise is to be provable on any host, which is a promise a compiler guard can keep and a pier-side guard cannot. Every rung's own count line moved 7 to 8, since a suite that says 7/7 while running eight is lying about its own roster.
obs Then the ledger's own guard, run because a round that touches the ledger should ask it, had been RED for a day. reds_ledger_monotone_witness asserted rows=121 as an EQUALITY, so it went red the moment %122 landed and stayed red through %123 and %124 while all three rounds closed green -- it sits in tools/gen/season/ and nothing called it. A count that grows belongs in a floor, never in an equality. The literal was guarding something real, since a spine that once read 73 of 101 and said ok is a genuine red; an equality simply guards the floor and forbids the growth in one stroke. Booked and closed as %126, with the refusal proven by lifting the floor above what the spine reaches.
obs REDS %123's lesson landed a second time in two days -- a witness on no roster is a witness nobody runs. That lantern has now fired twice, so the fix was structural rather than a re-pin.
obs The two new rows carried the living pin past its 24,576-byte bound, 24,417 to 30,345, and the ledger's completeness guard said so by name. Folded %122, %123, and %124 to crux/archive/ at 22,236 bytes -- the sixteenth such fold, routine and documented, nothing lost and each row one click away. No cairn: a fold accretes, and the cairn rule governs a debride that cuts.
obs Left main unlifted, deliberately and with the whole measurement standing. Standfast orders a red ahead of constructive work, and three reds opened in this lap's first probe. The fold is now fully surveyed rather than merely named -- 79 rungs, one lifted body, the widening priced across whole cohorts -- so the next lap starts from arithmetic rather than from a question.
obs The mutual import is legal now rather than recommended. Single-strandedness still argues for an import graph a reader can hold; the compiler simply no longer crashes when asked.
loom witness=rye_bridge_cycle_control cycle_files=2 deep_chain=1026 bound=1024 refusal=named staged_leftovers=0
loom suite=caravan_suite_witness rungs=107 verdict=GREEN compiler=fixed
loom ledger=crux/REDS.md bytes_before=30345 bytes_after=22236 bound=24576 rows_folded=3
file rye/src/main.rye the visited set marks on entry, a named bound refuses a runaway chain, and every file bridges under one name
file tools/rye_bridge_cycle_witness.rish the first guard this tree has ever pointed at its own compiler
file tools/fixtures/rye_bridge_cycle_control.sh a throwaway corpus whose answer is known by construction -- the cycle, the root reached from its dependency, the bound, the sweep
file tools/glow_tend_era_suite.rish the build bridge joins the roster as rung 8/8, so the guard is one nobody has to remember to run
file tools/gen/season/reds_ledger_monotone_witness.rish the row pin becomes a floor, keeping the shrink-guard and welcoming the climb
file crux/REDS.md %125 and %126 booked and closed, the header tally moved to the measured 125, three closed rows folded to archive
file crux/archive/REDS-namesake-exclusion-rows-122.md the namesake-exclusion round, folded whole
file crux/archive/REDS-positional-nib-read-rows-123.md the positional-read round, folded whole
file crux/archive/REDS-why-room-links-rows-124.md the why-room round, folded whole
recommend keep-going the main lift is measured and waiting, and it starts from arithmetic now rather than from a question -- 79 rungs across two cohorts differing by one qualifier, one lifted body serving both, the widening priced across whole cohorts at zero carry cost (run_self_test 97 distinct, run_dependent 68 in three families, fixed_argv_words a const the meter does not read), and the four base modules that blocked it unblocked by this lap's compiler fix. Expect roughly 1,075 lines off a carry standing at 87,795 against a ceiling of 87,800. Beside it the machine half of the address space stays owed and named -- page-table entry bytes, an ASID from seL4_ASIDPoolBits, the retype itself -- and the libsel4 generator gate (jinja2, ply) is still Keaton's word, still blocking rungs 3 and 4.
